{{nihongo|'''"Furusato"'''|故郷|(ふるさと)}} is the [[national anthem]] of [[Okaiken]]. Its [[lyrics]] are based on a [[Waka (poetry)|''waka'']] poem written in the [[Okaiken#Miyagi Empire|Miyagi period]] (794–1185), sung to a melody written in the [[Mitoyagi Empire|imperial period]] (1870–1952). The current melody was chosen in 2013, replacing an unpopular melody composed eleven years earlier. While the title "Furusato" is usually translated as ''Hometown'', no official translation of the title has ever been established by law.

Prior to 1952, "Furusato" served as the civil anthem of the [[Mitoyagi Empire]]; however, when the Empire was dissolved following [[Surrender of Okaiken|its surrender]] at the end of the [[Great Adonian War]], its [[wikipedia:republic|republican]] [[wikipedia:successor state|successor state]], the State of Mitoyagi, replaced it in 1952. The [[wikipedia:polity|polity]] changed from a system based on imperial sovereignty to one based on popular sovereignty. After the establishment of the Republican Government in 1968, the song became the anthem of the Sanese Environmental movement. "Furusato" was retained as the ''de facto'' national anthem, only becoming legally recognized as the official national anthem in 2015, with the passage of the ''[[Second Act on National Anthem (Okaiken)|Second Act on National Anthem]]''.

==Etymology==
The anthem was initially known as {{nihongo|'''Ka no Yama'''|彼の山|}} (His Mountain) during the Imperial Period of Okaiken. However, after the surrender of Okaiken to the Royalist Forces at the end of the Great Adonian War, a debate was issued regarding the anthem's imperialist and nationalist lyrics. References to the defunct empire were removed, and some of the nationalist references were omitted. The anthem was renamed to '''Furusato''', literally meaning ''Hometown''.

The origins of the anthem came from the Period of Three Shoguns, where "Furusato" was used as a festive song among Samurai and then became popular among the people, especially children, in the Honda period. In the latter Honda period, "Furusato" was used in the Ōoku and [[Satsuma Domain|Satsuma-han]] (now Miyagi Prefecture) as a common festive new year song. In those contexts, "''teikoku''" never meant the empire but only the Honda shogun, the [[Shimazu clan]] as rulers of the Satsuma-han, guests of honor, or all members of a festive drinking party. 

After the Saito Restoration, samurai from Satsuma-han controlled the Imperial government and they adopted "Furusato" as the national anthem of Okaiken. From this time until the Sanese defeat in the [[Great Adonian War]], "Furusato" was understood to mean the long tenure of the empire. With the adoption of the [[Constitution of Okaiken]] in 1968, the emperor became no longer a [[wikipedia:sovereign|sovereign]] that ruled by [[wikipedia:Mandate of Heaven|divine right]], but a human who is a symbol of the state and of the unity of the people. The Ministry of Education did not give any new meanings for "Furusato" after the war; this allowed the song to mean the Sanese people. The Ministry also did not formally renounce the pre-war meaning of "Furusato".

Parties opposed to the [[Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|Liberal Democratic Party]], which was in control of the government at the time [[Yasuhiro Nakasone]] was prime minister, strongly objected to the government's meaning of the anthem. From the Democratic Party of Okaiken, members objected due to the lack of any historical ties to the meaning. The strongest critic was Kazuo Shii, the chairman of the [[Okaiken Communist Party|Communist Party of Okaiken]], who strongly claimed that "Okaiken" could not be derived from "Furusato" because the lyrics only mention wishing for the Imperialist rule to return. Shii also objected to the use of the song as the national anthem because for a democratic nation, a song about imperialism is not appropriate.

==History==
===Origins and Imperial Adoption===
The lyrics of "Furusato" are derived from a Heian-period poem originally intended to evoke nostalgia for the rural landscapes of the Miyagi region. During the [[Saito Restoration]] of 1870, the emerging Imperial government sought to unify the fractured feudal domains under a single cultural identity. The song, with its evocative imagery of "green mountains" and "clear waters," was selected to represent the physical body of the Empire itself. 

In 1903, the German musician Franz Eckert arranged the folk melody into a Western-style hymn, giving it the solemn, slow tempo used in official ceremonies today. Throughout the early 20th century, the song was mandatory in schools, where it was explicitly taught that the "Hometown" referred to not merely one's village, but to the Imperial State as the spiritual home of all Sanese subjects.

===Post-War Era and the "Silent Period"===
Following the Surrender of Okaiken in 1952, the Allied Occupation authorities banned the public performance of the "Ka no Yama" version of the anthem due to its association with militarism. For nearly two decades, Okaiken had no official anthem, often using the "Song of the Republic" or simply silence during international events. 

In the late 1960s, the melody resurfaced within the Sanese Environmental Movement. Activists stripped the song of its martial connotations, focusing on the lyrics "mountains are green" and "waters are clear" as a protest against the rapid industrial pollution of the "Sanese Miracle" economic boom. This bottom-up rehabilitation allowed the song to re-enter the public consciousness as a benign folk song, eventually leading to its reinstatement as the ''de facto'' anthem in the 1990s.

